": [{\"coord\": [1454, 94, 1494, 127], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"95\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [384, 207, 1242, 255], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Airport and Airway Trust Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 269, 794, 514], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"The Airport and Airway Trust Fund was established on the books of the Department of Treasury in fiscal year 1971, according to provisions of the Airport and Airway Revenue Act of 1970 [49 United States Code 1742(a), repealed]. The Tax Equity and Fiscal Responsibility Act of 1982 (Public Law 97-248, dated September 3, 1982) reestablished the trust fund in the Internal Revenue Code (26 United States Code 9502) effective September 1, 1982.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 514, 796, 759], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Treasury transfers from the general fund to the trust fund amounts equivalent to the taxes received from transportation of persons and property by air, gasoline and jet fuel used in commercial and noncommercial aircraft, and an international arrival and departure tax. The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990 (Public Law 101-508, dated November 5, 1990) increased rates for the excise taxes transferred to the fund.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 759, 798, 971], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Treasury bases these transfers on estimates made by the Secretary of the Treasury. These are subject to adjustments in later transfers in the amount of actual tax receipts. The FAA Reauthorization Act of 2018 (Public Law 115-254), effective October 5, 2018, extended the aviation excise taxes until October 1, 2023. The Act included provisions that:\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 983, 794, 1228], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Retained the existing passenger ticket, flight segment, and freight waybill taxes. The domestic flight segment tax is indexed to the Consumer Price Index; effective calendar year 2021, the tax is $4.30. It also retained a special rule applied to flights between the continental United States and Alaska or Hawaii. This departure tax is indexed to the Consumer Price Index; effective calendar year 2021, the tax is $9.60.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 1233, 790, 1360], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Retained the existing tax per person for international flights that begin or end in the United States. The tax is indexed to the Consumer Price Index; effective calendar year 2021, the tax is $19.10.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 1365, 798, 1546], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Retained the existing tax on payments to airlines for frequent flyer and similar awards by banks and credit card companies, merchants and frequent flyer program partners, such as other airlines, hotels and rental car companies, and other businesses. The tax on mileage awards is 7.5% of the value of the miles.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 273, 1494, 434], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Retained the commercial aviation fuel tax and the general aviation jet fuel/gas taxes. The current tax rate for commercial aviation fuel is 4.3 cents per gallon and 19.3 cents per gallon for general aviation gas. General aviation jet fuel is 21.8 cents per gallon.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 434, 1495, 676], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"The FAA Modernization and Reform Act 2012 imposed a new surtax on fuel used in aircraft that is part of a fractional ownership program; the surtax applies to fuel used after March 31, 2012. Currently this is a 14.1 cents per gallon surcharge of fuel used in fractional ownership flights. It also changed the classification of transportation as part of a fractional ownership program from commercial aviation to noncommercial aviation.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [840, 676, 1494, 952], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"The FAA Modernization and Reform Act 2012 also repealed the excise tax exemption for transportation by small jet aircraft operating on non-established lines. (IRS defines the term \\\"operated on an established line\\\" to mean operated with some degree of regularity between definite points). This was changed by the Tax Cuts and Jobs act (Public Law 115-97), which exempts certain payments related to the management of private aircraft from the excise taxes imposed on taxable transportation by air.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [836, 952, 1494, 1320], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"More recently, the Coronavirus Aid, Relief, and Economic Security (CARES) Act (Public Law 116-136) enacted in late March suspended most of the aviation excise taxes through calendar year 2020 in order to provide some financial relief to the struggling commercial aviation industry. While fuel taxes paid by General Aviation users remained in effect, during this period Treasury did not collect taxes on fuel used by Commercial users, as well as taxes on commercial passengers and cargo. Combined with lower levels of aviation activity caused by the pandemic, this \\\"tax holiday\\\" significantly reduced the tax collections for FY 2020, thereby lowering the AATF balance.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [836, 1320, 1495, 1532], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"Treasury makes available to the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), Department of Transportation (DOT), amounts required for outlays to carry out the Airport and Airway program. The Secretary of the Treasury makes other charges to the trust fund to transfer certain refunds of taxes and certain outlays, under section 34 of the Internal Revenue Code (IRC).\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1373, 1992, 1494, 2018], \"page_id\": 101}], \"content\": \"March 2021\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1448, 92, 1488, 132], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"96\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [481, 189, 1162, 271], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"TABLE TF-1.\\u2014Airport and Airway Trust Fund Results of Operations, Fiscal Year 2020\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 278, 1497, 1304], \"page_id\": 102}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th>Description</th><th>IRC section (26 United States Code)</th><th>Amount</th></tr><tr><td>Balance Oct. 1, 2019</td><td></td><td>$17,915,697,037</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts:</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Grants-in-aid for Airports funding from General Fund</td><td></td><td>10,400,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Excise taxes (transferred from general fund):</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Liquid fuel in a fractional ownership flight</td><td>4043</td><td>15,172,380</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid fuel other than gasoline</td><td>4041</td><td>346,204,494</td></tr><tr><td>Gasoline</td><td>4081</td><td>28,482,165</td></tr><tr><td>Transportation by airseats, berths, etc.</td><td>4261 (a) (b)</td><td>6,497,250,713</td></tr><tr><td>Use of international travel facilities</td><td>4261 (c)</td><td>1,847,101,558</td></tr><tr><td>Transportation of property, cargo</td><td>4271</td><td>300,478,363</td></tr><tr><td>Gross excise taxes</td><td></td><td>9,034,689,673</td></tr><tr><td>Less refunds of taxes (reimbursed to general fund):</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Liquid fuel other than gasoline</td><td>4041